@chapter Burgermistress Frontend Language Reference
@cindex Burgermistress frontend
@cindex frontend, Burgermistress

This chapter provides a comprehensive reference for the Burgermistress frontend language as
recognized by the lexer and parser in the Eightbol toolchain. Burgermistress is a hybrid
language combining BASIC-like syntax with Prolog-style logic programming constructs. It is
designed for scripting game events, dialogues, and AI behaviors on 8-bit and 16-bit systems.

@menu
* Lexical Conventions::
* Variables and Types::
* Control Flow Statements::
* Subroutines and Functions::
* Dialogue Statements::
* Input/Output Statements::
* Prolog-Style Goals::
* Operators::
* Identifier Normalization::
* Unsupported Features::
@end menu


@node Lexical Conventions
@section Lexical Conventions

Burgermistress source code consists of statements separated by newlines. Comments begin
with an apostrophe (@code{'}) at the start of a line and extend to the end of the line.

@example
' This is a comment
PRINT "Hello"  ' Inline comment not supported; apostrophe must start line
@end example

@subsubsection Whitespace

Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) separates tokens. Multiple spaces are treated as
single separators.

@subsubsection Number Literals

Burgermistress supports multiple number literal formats:

@table @asis
@item Decimal — Plain digits: @code{42}, @code{0}, @code{255}
@item Hexadecimal — Prefix @code{&h} or @code{0x}: @code{&hFF}, @code{0xFF}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}
@item Octal — Prefix @code{&o} or @code{0o}: @code{&o77}, @code{0o755}
@item Binary — Prefix @code{&b} or @code{0b}: @code{&b1010}, @code{0b11110000}
@item Dword — Prefix @code{&d"WORD"} or @code{0d"WORD"}: @code{&d"12345678"}
@end table

Examples in equivalent forms:
@example
255        ' Decimal
&hFF       ' Hexadecimal
0xFF       ' Hexadecimal (alternate)
&o377      ' Octal
0b11111111 ' Binary
&d"255"    ' Dword
@end example

@subsubsection String Literals

String literals are enclosed in double quotes: @code{"text"}. The lexer scans until the
matching quote.

@example
"Hello, World!"
"Line one"
@end example

Single quotes are reserved for comments, not strings.

@subsubsection Atoms

Atoms are symbolic constants used as predicate names, function names, and symbolic values.
They begin with a lowercase letter or underscore.

@example
hello
_private
item_sword
@end example

@subsubsection Identifiers

Identifiers follow Header-Case convention (first letter uppercase, each word capitalized).
They are normalized from source input to ensure consistent naming.

@node Variables and Types
@section Variables and Types

@menu
* Variable Declaration::
* Primitive Types::
* Assignment::
@end menu

@node Variable Declaration
@subsection Variable Declaration

@subheading Purpose and Function

The @code{DIM} statement declares one or more variables, optionally specifying their
type.

@subheading Syntax

@example
DIM variableName
DIM variableName AS type
DIM var1, var2, var3
DIM var1 AS type1, var2 AS type2
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
DIM counter
DIM score AS INTEGER
DIM playerName AS STRING
DIM x, y, z AS INTEGER
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

Variables must be valid identifiers following Header-Case convention.

@node Primitive Types
@subsection Primitive Types

@multitable @columnfractions 0.3 0.7
@headitem Type @tab Description
@item @code{INTEGER} @tab Signed integer (typically 16-bit on 8-bit targets, 32-bit on 16-bit)
@item @code{STRING} @tab Text string (variable length, up to 255 bytes)
@item @code{BOOLEAN} @tab Boolean value (true/false)
@item @code{DOUBLE} @tab 32-bit fixed-point or floating-point (limited support)
@end multitable

@node Assignment
@subsection Assignment

@subheading Purpose and Function

Assignment stores a value into a variable using the equals operator.

@subheading Syntax

@example
variableName = expression
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
counter = 0
score = score + 10
playerName = "Hero"
isAlive = TRUE
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:move} node.

@node Control Flow Statements
@section Control Flow Statements

@menu
* IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF::
* WHILE/WEND::
* FOR/NEXT::
* DO/LOOP/UNTIL::
* EXIT::
@end menu

@node IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF
@subsection IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF

@subheading Purpose and Function

The @code{IF} statement evaluates a condition and executes one of two statement branches.

@subheading Syntax

@example
IF condition THEN statement

IF condition THEN
    statements
ENDIF

IF condition THEN
    statements
ELSE
    statements
ENDIF
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
IF x > 0 THEN x = x - 1

IF health <= 0 THEN
    isAlive = FALSE
    DISPLAY "You died!"
ENDIF

IF score > highScore THEN
    highScore = score
    DISPLAY "New high score!"
ELSE
    DISPLAY "Try again!"
ENDIF
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@var{condition} is any Boolean expression using comparison operators (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=)
or logical operators (AND, OR, NOT).

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:if} node.

@node WHILE/WEND
@subsection WHILE/WEND

@subheading Purpose and Function

The @code{WHILE} statement loops while a condition is true. The condition is evaluated at
the start of each iteration.

@subheading Syntax

@example
WHILE condition :
    statements
WEND
@end example

Note: A colon (:) separates the condition from the body statements.

@subheading Examples

@example
WHILE x < 10 :
    x = x + 1
WEND

WHILE isRunning :
    UPDATE_GAME()
    IF quitFlag THEN
        isRunning = FALSE
    ENDIF
WEND
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:perform :until} construct.

@node FOR/NEXT
@subsection FOR/NEXT

@subheading Purpose and Function

The @code{FOR} statement iterates a variable from a start value to an end value. The loop
variable is automatically incremented by 1 after each iteration (or by the specified STEP).

@subheading Syntax

@example
FOR variable = start TO end :
    statements
NEXT

FOR variable = start TO end STEP step :
    statements
NEXT
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
FOR i = 1 TO 10 :
    DISPLAY i
NEXT

FOR j = 0 TO 255 STEP 2 :
    POKE j, 0
NEXT

FOR k = 100 TO 1 STEP -1 :
    COUNTDOWN()
NEXT
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item @var{variable} must be a previously declared variable or simple identifier
@item @var{start}, @var{end}, and @var{step} are integer expressions
@item @var{step} defaults to 1 if omitted
@item @var{step} may be negative for countdown loops
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:perform :varying} construct.

@node DO/LOOP/UNTIL
@subsection DO/LOOP/UNTIL

@subheading Purpose and Function

The @code{DO/LOOP/UNTIL} statement is a post-test loop. The body executes at least once;
the condition is evaluated after each iteration.

@subheading Syntax

@example
DO :
    statements
LOOP UNTIL condition
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
DO :
    guess = guess + 1
LOOP UNTIL guess = answer

DO :
    GET_INPUT()
LOOP UNTIL inputReady
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:perform :until} construct with post-test semantics.

@node EXIT
@subsection EXIT

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{EXIT} terminates the current subroutine or function. Control returns to the
caller.

@subheading Syntax

@example
EXIT
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
IF error THEN
    EXIT
ENDIF

FOR i = 1 TO 100 :
    IF FOUND(i) THEN
        result = i
        EXIT
    ENDIF
NEXT
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:exit-method} or @code{:go-back} node.

@node Subroutines and Functions
@section Subroutines and Functions

@menu
* SUB/END SUB::
* FUNCTION/END FUNCTION::
* CALL Statement::
* GOSUB Statement::
* RETURN Statement::
@end menu

@node SUB/END SUB
@subsection SUB/END SUB

@subheading Purpose and Function

A @code{SUB} defines a subroutine—a named block of statements that can be called and
executed from elsewhere in the program. Subroutines do not return a value.

@subheading Syntax

@example
SUB name :
    statements
END SUB
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
SUB INITIALIZE :
    score = 0
    lives = 3
    level = 1
END SUB

SUB UPDATE_SCORE(points) :
    score = score + points
    IF score > highScore THEN
        highScore = score
    ENDIF
END SUB
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:method} node.

@node FUNCTION/END FUNCTION
@subsection FUNCTION/END FUNCTION

@subheading Purpose and Function

A @code{FUNCTION} defines a named function that returns a value. The function is invoked
as part of an expression and produces a result.

@subheading Syntax

@example
FUNCTION name :
    statements
END FUNCTION
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
FUNCTION MAX(a, b) :
    IF a > b THEN
        RETURN a
    ELSE
        RETURN b
    ENDIF
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ABS(x) :
    IF x < 0 THEN
        RETURN -x
    ELSE
        RETURN x
    ENDIF
END FUNCTION
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:method} node with return value semantics.

@node CALL Statement
@subsection CALL Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{CALL} invokes a subroutine by name.

@subheading Syntax

@example
CALL subroutineName
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
CALL INITIALIZE
CALL UPDATE_DISPLAY
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:call} node.

@node GOSUB Statement
@subsection GOSUB Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{GOSUB} jumps to a subroutine and returns via @code{RETURN}. This is a legacy
BASIC construct.

@subheading Syntax

@example
GOSUB labelName
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
GOSUB CLEANUP
@end example

@node RETURN Statement
@subsection RETURN Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{RETURN} returns from a @code{GOSUB} or exits a @code{FUNCTION}.

@subheading Syntax

@example
RETURN
RETURN expression
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
RETURN
RETURN resultValue
RETURN x + y * z
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:go-back} node.

@node Dialogue Statements
@section Dialogue Statements

@menu
* DIALOGUE Statement::
* Prolog-Style Goals::
@end menu

@node DIALOGUE Statement
@subsection DIALOGUE Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{DIALOGUE} displays character speech or narrative text. It supports Prolog-style
goal predicates for complex dialogue logic.

@subheading Syntax

@example
DIALOGUE speakerName : goals
DIALOGUE speakerName : predicate(arg1, arg2, ...)
DIALOGUE speakerName : goal1, goal2, goal3
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
DIALOGUE Merchant : GREET()
DIALOGUE Hero : SAY("I need a sword")
DIALOGUE Narrator : DESCRIBE(SCENE, LOCATION), EMOTION(MOOD)
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item @var{speakerName} is an identifier (the character speaking)
@item @var{goals} are one or more Prolog-style predicates separated by commas
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:dialogue} node.

@node Prolog-Style Goals
@subsection Prolog-Style Goals

@subheading Purpose and Function

Goals are Prolog-style predicates that can be used within @code{DIALOGUE} statements or
as standalone calls. They enable logic programming patterns for game AI and dialogue
systems.

@subheading Syntax

@example
predicateName
predicateName(arg1, arg2, ...)
goal1, goal2, goal3
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
DIALOGUE NPC : HAS_QUEST(), NOT_COMPLETED(QUEST)
DIALOGUE Player : INVENTORY_COUNT(Items), Items > 0
@end example

@subheading Available Goal Types

The frontend supports the following built-in goal patterns:

@table @asis
@item @code{HAS_ITEM(character, item)} — Check if character has item
@item @code{CAN_TALK(speaker)} — Check if speaker can talk
@item @code{EMOTION(mood)} — Set emotional state
@item @code{DESCRIBE(scene, location)} — Describe scene/location
@item @code{QUEST_STATUS(quest, status)} — Check quest status
@end table

@node Input/Output Statements
@section Input/Output Statements

@menu
* PRINT Statement::
* INPUT Statement::
@end menu

@node PRINT Statement
@subsection PRINT Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{PRINT} outputs text or values to the console or output stream.

@subheading Syntax

@example
PRINT expression, expression, ...
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
PRINT "Hello, World!"
PRINT "Score: ", score
PRINT "X=", x, " Y=", y
PRINT a, b, c
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:print} node.

@node INPUT Statement
@subsection INPUT Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{INPUT} reads values from the user into variables. An optional prompt can be displayed.

@subheading Syntax

@example
INPUT variableName
INPUT variable1, variable2, ...
INPUT variableName WITH "prompt"
INPUT variable1, variable2 WITH "prompt"
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
INPUT name
INPUT x, y
INPUT guess WITH "Enter your guess: "
INPUT firstName, lastName WITH "Enter your name: "
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item Variables must be previously declared or simple identifiers
@item Prompt string is optional
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:input} node with optional prompt.

@node Operators
@section Operators

@menu
* Arithmetic Operators::
* Relational Operators::
* Logical Operators::
* Bitwise Shift Operators::
* Operator Precedence::
@end menu

@node Arithmetic Operators
@subsection Arithmetic Operators

@table @code
@item +
Addition. Example: @code{x + y}

@item -
Subtraction. Example: @code{a - b}

@item *
Multiplication. Example: @code{width * height}

@item /
Division. Example: @code{total / count}
@end table

@node Relational Operators
@subsection Relational Operators

@table @code
@item =
Equality. True if operands are equal. Example: @code{x = y}

@item <>
Inequality. True if operands differ. Example: @code{x <> y}

@item <
Less than. Example: @code{health < 0}

@item >
Greater than. Example: @code{score > 100}

@item <=
Less than or equal. Example: @code{x <= 10}

@item >=
Greater than or equal. Example: @code{level >= 5}
@end table

@node Logical Operators
@subsection Logical Operators

@table @code
@item AND
Logical conjunction. Example: @code{x > 0 AND y > 0}

@item OR
Logical disjunction. Example: @code{health = 0 OR lives = 0}

@item NOT
Logical negation. Example: @code{NOT isDead}
@end table

@node Bitwise Shift Operators
@subsection Bitwise Shift Operators

@table @code
@item <<
Left shift. Example: @code{x << 2}

@item >>
Right shift. Example: @code{y >> 1}
@end table

@node Operator Precedence
@subsection Operator Precedence

Operator precedence (lowest to highest):
@enumerate
@item Logical OR
@item Logical AND
@item NOT
@item Relational (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=)
@item Bitwise shift (<<, >>)
@item Addition and subtraction (+, -)
@item Multiplication and division (*, /)
@end enumerate

Parentheses may be used to override precedence.

@node Identifier Normalization
@section Identifier Normalization

The Burgermistress frontend automatically normalizes all identifiers to Header-Case format
(first letter uppercase, each subsequent word capitalized). Dots are preserved for nested
access patterns (e.g., @code{My.Field}).

@subheading Normalization Rules

@itemize
@item The first character is converted to uppercase.
@item Subsequent alphabetic characters are converted to lowercase unless preceded by a
dot.
@item Dots are preserved to delimit nested fields.
@item Underscores are converted to hyphens.
@end itemize

@subheading Examples

@multitable @columnfractions 0.3 0.3 0.4
@headitem Source @tab Normalized @tab Notes
@item @code{myVariable} @tab @code{MyVariable} @tab camelCase to Header-Case
@item @code{MY_FUNCTION} @tab @code{My-function} @tab Underscores become hyphens
@item @code{player.score} @tab @code{Player.score} @tab Nested field access
@item @code{HTTPServer} @tab @code{Httpsserver} @tab All lowercase after first
@end multitable

@node Unsupported Features
@section Unsupported Features

The Burgermistress frontend intentionally omits certain features to match EightBol's
8-bit/16-bit target constraints:

@itemize
@item Object-oriented programming features (classes, inheritance)
@item Exception handling (TRY/CATCH/FINALLY)
@item Structured exception handling
@item Lambda expressions and closures
@item First-class functions
@item Regular expressions
@item File I/O beyond basic INPUT/PRINT
@item Dynamic memory allocation (arrays must be fixed-size)
@item Recursive function calls (limited stack on 8-bit targets)
@item Floating-point literals (use INTEGER or scaled arithmetic)
@item Complex number support
@item Bit field declarations
@item Pointer arithmetic
@item Multiple statements on one line (must use separate lines)
@item Line numbers (use labels for GOSUB)
@item DATA/READ statements (use copybooks instead)
@end itemize

For equivalent functionality, consult the EightBol COBOL-style constructs that provide
similar capabilities with appropriate code generation for constrained systems.

@node File Extensions
@section File Extensions

Burgermistress source files use the extension @code{.bmrs}. As an undocumented
alternative, the extension @code{.rah} is also accepted (treated identically to
@code{.bmrs}) but is not officially supported.

@example
game-script.bmrs
dialogue-tree.bmrs
@end example

@node Summary
@section Summary

This chapter has documented the complete Burgermistress frontend language:

@itemize
@item Variable declaration: @code{DIM} with optional type (@code{AS INTEGER}, etc.)
@item Assignment: @code{variable = expression}
@item Control flow: @code{IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF}, @code{WHILE/WEND}, @code{FOR/NEXT},
@code{DO/LOOP/UNTIL}, @code{EXIT}
@item Subroutines: @code{SUB/END SUB}, @code{CALL}, @code{GOSUB}
@item Functions: @code{FUNCTION/END FUNCTION}, @code{RETURN}
@item Dialogue: @code{DIALOGUE} with Prolog-style goals
@item I/O: @code{PRINT}, @code{INPUT} with optional prompts
@item Operators: arithmetic (+, -, *, /), relational (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=), logical
(AND, OR, NOT), bitwise (<<, >>)
@item Number formats: decimal, hexadecimal, octal, binary, dword
@item Identifier normalization: Header-Case
@end itemize

For implementation details, see @file{src/frontend-burgermistress/burger-lexer.lisp} and
@file{src/frontend-burgermistress/burger-parser.lisp}.
